# The Software Development Life Cycle: From Idea to Maintenance

> Writing a 100-line script needs inspiration; delivering a
> million-line system needs engineering discipline. The Software
> Development Life Cycle (SDLC) is that discipline — a sequence of
> phases that turns a vague need into working, maintained software
> while keeping cost, schedule and quality under control.

## 1. Why a Process at All

[definition]
The **Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)** is a structured
sequence of phases — requirement analysis, design, implementation,
testing, deployment and maintenance — that guides a software project
from conception to retirement. Its purpose is predictability: catching
mistakes early, when they are cheapest to fix.
[/definition]

The economics drive everything: an error that costs 1 unit to fix
during requirements can cost 10× during coding, 100× during testing
and 1000× after release. Every process model below is a different
strategy for moving error discovery toward the cheap end.

## 2. The Core Phases

| Phase | Central question | Key outputs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Requirement analysis | What must the system do? | Software Requirement Specification (SRS) |
| 2. Design | How will it do it? | Architecture, data models, interface designs (HLD + LLD) |
| 3. Implementation | Build it | Source code, unit-tested modules |
| 4. Testing | Does it work correctly? | Test reports, defect logs |
| 5. Deployment | Deliver it | Installed/launched product, user training |
| 6. Maintenance | Keep it working | Patches, enhancements, retirements |

Two requirements vocabularies matter in exams: **functional**
requirements describe what the system does ("user can reset
password"), while **non-functional** requirements constrain how well
it does it (performance, security, usability, scalability).

## 3. The Waterfall Model

Phases flow strictly downward, each finishing before the next begins,
with signed-off documents at every step.

- **Strengths:** simple, well-documented, easy to manage; suits stable,
  well-understood domains (regulated industries, embedded firmware).
- **Weaknesses:** late feedback — the customer sees working software
  only at the end, and a wrong early assumption is catastrophically
  expensive to unwind.

[example]
A payments switch for a bank regulator might choose Waterfall because
every requirement must be frozen and audited. A social-media startup
would not — its features change faster than documents can be signed.
[/example]

## 4. Iterative and Incremental Models

**Iterative** models revisit the same phases repeatedly, refining the
product each cycle. **Incremental** models deliver the system in
usable slices — the first increment might implement only login and
profile, the next adds payments.

- **Spiral model (Boehm):** each loop through the spiral covers
  planning, risk analysis, engineering and evaluation. Its signature
  is **explicit risk handling at every iteration** — best for large,
  high-risk projects.
- **RAD (Rapid Application Development):** 60–90 day cycles, heavy
  user involvement, reusable components and prototyping; needs strong
  staffing and modularised systems.
- **Prototyping:** build a quick throwaway model to clarify unclear
  requirements before committing to real development.

## 5. Agile and the V-Model

[definition]
**Agile** embraces changing requirements through short, fixed-length
iterations (sprints), continuous customer collaboration and working
software as the primary measure of progress. The **Agile Manifesto**
values individuals and interactions, working software, customer
collaboration and responding to change — each over rigid processes,
comprehensive documentation, contract negotiation and following a
plan.
[/definition]

Scrum operationalises agile with three roles (product owner, scrum
master, developers), five events (sprint, planning, daily stand-up,
review, retrospective) and three artefacts (product backlog, sprint
backlog, increment). Kanban limits work-in-progress on a visible
board instead of time-boxing.

The **V-Model** pairs each development phase with a matching test
level:

| Development phase | Verified by |
| --- | --- |
| Requirements | Acceptance testing |
| System design | System testing |
| High-level design | Integration testing |
| Low-level design | Unit testing |
| Implementation | coding, executed alongside unit tests |

[important]
A favourite exam distinction: **verification** asks "are we building
the product right?" (matching specifications — reviews, inspections);
**validation** asks "are we building the right product?" (matching
user needs — actual testing). The V-Model is strong on verification;
agile's customer collaboration strengthens validation.
[/important]

## 6. Maintenance: The Longest Phase

More money is spent maintaining software than building it — typically
60–80% of lifetime cost. Four categories:

1. **Corrective** — fixing discovered defects.
2. **Adaptive** — adjusting to changed environments (new OS, new tax
   rules).
3. **Perfective** — improving performance or adding refinements
   users ask for; the largest share in practice.
4. **Preventive** — refactoring and paying down technical debt to
   make future change cheaper.

## 7. Choosing a Model

| Situation | Best-fit model |
| --- | --- |
| Fixed, regulated, well-understood needs | Waterfall |
| Large project dominated by risk | Spiral |
| Unclear requirements, need quick feedback | Prototyping |
| Fast delivery with strong user involvement | RAD / Agile |
| Safety-critical, contract-driven | V-Model |

No model is universally correct; maturity lies in matching process to
project volatility, risk profile and team culture. That judgment —
not memorising diagram shapes — is what the exam questions ultimately
probe.
